"""
The users of {{.ProjectName}}: who can sign in, with which roles.

YOURS. forge scaffolds this file once and never rewrites it.

Every env imports this ONE declaration (as it imports workloads.k), so dev,
staging and prod declare the same roles and applications. Only WHERE the
identity runs differs, and each env's main.k says that:

  dev      `forge.IdentityOnHost {infra = "idp"}`: the dev IdP `forge env up`
           starts. `forge env up` converges this declaration on it with the
           same library the control plane runs, and fills the API's
           jwt_issuer, jwt_audience, oidc_client_id, idp_base and
           idp_broker_token for you (forge.IdentityRef): nothing is copied
           by hand and no config.k line names them.
  staging  hosted (the default): the control plane gives the env its own
  prod     identity instance and delivers the same values.

The application (the API's sign-in) and the consumers (every service
workload) are DERIVED from the workloads the env binds. Add what you need:

    roles = [
        forge.IdentityRole {key = "admin", members_from = "declared", members = ["you@example.com"]}
    ]
    machine_users = [...]      # non-human accounts and their keys
    seed_users = [...]         # dev/ephemeral envs only

Bringing your own issuer instead (Auth0, Okta, ...)? Declare no `identity` in
that env's main.k and set jwt_issuer / oidc_client_id in its config.k.
`forge skill load deploy/managed-identity` has every field.
"""

import forge

IDENTITY = forge.ManagedIdentity {
    roles = [
        forge.IdentityRole {key = "admin", display_name = "Administrators"}
        forge.IdentityRole {key = "member", display_name = "Members"}
    ]
}
